 1.2 For an active block of size $r\ge2$, a Francis sweep has at most $\max(r-2,0)+1$ initial/chase reflectors of length two or three. Each acts on a bounded number of rows and columns across at most $n$ entries. The active block sizes sum to at most $n$, so a full sweep and its non-controller deflation/status work cost $O(n^2)$; for $n=1$ there is no active sweep. The linear sweep budgets therefore give $O(n^3)$ matrix work for the two Schur calls. The same bound covers materializing the recorded factor products: initialization contributes $O(n^3)$ and each bounded-support chase factor updates only a bounded number of dense-factor columns, at cost $O(n)$. [F1, F2, given, algebra]
 
-1.3 The backward-error accumulator starts with one outward Frobenius norm of the input, costing $O(n^2)$ by the declared entrywise evaluator model. Each similarity uses a fixed number of scalar upper-bound calls once its local coefficient is evaluated. The explicit real/complex coefficient formulas require $O(m)$ scalar operations on active dimension $m$: their dimension-dependent powers can be formed by repeated multiplication and a square root for a half-integer exponent. Thus the at most $n$ initialization coefficients cost $O(n^2)$ in total, while each length-two/three chase coefficient costs $O(1)$. There are $O(n^2)$ chase similarities over the linear number of sweeps. Each recorded one-entry deflation has Frobenius norm equal to the modulus of its signed stored value, evaluated from that one-entry list with $O(1)$ scalar work; at most $O(n)$ such writes occur per sweep, hence $O(n^2)$ in total. Each stage then makes a fixed number of recurrence calls. Unavailable coefficient/evaluator results only stop certificate bookkeeping. Consequently this entire ancillary accumulation costs $O(n^2)$ and fits inside the cubic matrix-work bound, including failure branches. [F1, F2, step 1.1, step 1.2, given, algebra]
 
